{%- comment -%}
  Gift-card recipient — who the card is actually for.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The theme had no `product.gift_card?` branch anywhere, so
  selling a gift card behaved like selling a jumper: Shopify emails the code to
  whoever paid. A shopper buying a present for someone else got the present
  themselves and had to forward a code, and nothing on the page warned them
  that was going to happen. Shopify's recipient fields are the supported fix,
  and they only work if the theme renders them inside the product form.

  The fields are named exactly as Shopify's cart expects — `properties[...]` —
  and are wrapped in a disabled fieldset until the shopper opts in, because a
  disabled control posts nothing at all. That is the whole mechanism: opt out
  and the card behaves as before; opt in and Shopify handles the delivery, the
  message and the send date itself.

  THE FOUR VISIBLE FIELDS ARE NOT ENOUGH, and shipping them alone reproduced the
  exact bug this snippet was written to fix. Recipient delivery is switched on by
  a CONTROL property, `__shopify_send_gift_card_to_recipient`; without it Shopify
  treats "Recipient email" as an ordinary line-item property — decorative text on
  the order — and still emails the code to the buyer. The shopper fills in a date
  picker and a message, sees them confirmed on the order, and the present goes to
  the wrong person anyway, which is worse than not offering the fields at all.

  `__shopify_offset` is the second half: "Send on" is a bare date, so without the
  browser's UTC offset Shopify reads it in the SHOP's timezone and a card can
  arrive a day early or late. Both are hidden inputs inside the same fieldset, so
  the disabled-means-not-submitted mechanism still governs them exactly as it
  governs the visible fields. Properties prefixed `__` are hidden from the
  shopper and from the order page by Shopify's own convention.

  Usage: {%- render 'pdp-gift-card', product: product -%}   (inside the form)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if product.gift_card? -%}
  {%- assign gc_id = 'gc-' | append: product.id -%}
  <div class="gc" data-gift-card>
    <label class="gc__toggle" for="{{ gc_id }}-on">
      <input class="obs-sr gc__on" id="{{ gc_id }}-on" type="checkbox" data-gift-card-toggle>
      <span class="gc__switch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="gc__toggle-label">{{ 'product.gift_card.toggle' | t }}</span>
    </label>

    {%- comment -%} `disabled` is load-bearing: a disabled field is not
    submitted, so an un-opted-in card posts no recipient and behaves exactly as
    it did before this snippet existed. {%- endcomment -%}
    <fieldset class="gc__fields" data-gift-card-fields disabled>
      <legend class="gc__legend lbl-sm">{{ 'product.gift_card.heading' | t }}</legend>

      {%- comment -%} The control property. Everything below is inert without it:
      Shopify only switches a gift card into recipient delivery when this is
      present, and `if_present` is the literal value it looks for. {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="properties[__shopify_send_gift_card_to_recipient]" value="if_present">

      {%- comment -%} Filled in by theme.js with the browser's UTC offset so the
      send date is read in the SHOPPER's timezone rather than the shop's. Empty
      is safe — Shopify falls back to the shop's — so no-JS degrades to the old
      behaviour instead of breaking the form. {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="properties[__shopify_offset]" value="" data-gift-card-offset>

      <div class="gc__row">
        <label class="lbl-sm" for="{{ gc_id }}-email">{{ 'product.gift_card.email' | t }}</label>
        <input id="{{ gc_id }}-email" type="email" name="properties[Recipient email]" autocomplete="off" required>
      </div>

      <div class="gc__row">
        <label class="lbl-sm" for="{{ gc_id }}-name">{{ 'product.gift_card.name' | t }}</label>
        <input id="{{ gc_id }}-name" type="text" name="properties[Recipient name]" autocomplete="off">
      </div>

      <div class="gc__row">
        <label class="lbl-sm" for="{{ gc_id }}-message">{{ 'product.gift_card.message' | t }}</label>
        <textarea id="{{ gc_id }}-message" name="properties[Message]" rows="3" maxlength="200"></textarea>
      </div>

      <div class="gc__row">
        <label class="lbl-sm" for="{{ gc_id }}-send">{{ 'product.gift_card.send_on' | t }}</label>
        <input id="{{ gc_id }}-send" type="date" name="properties[Send on]">
        <p class="gc__note stmt-sm mute">{{ 'product.gift_card.send_on_note' | t }}</p>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <p class="gc__fallback stmt-sm mute" data-gift-card-fallback>{{ 'product.gift_card.note' | t }}</p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
